[s0l0!-header][h1]The malloc Function[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]malloc()[/b] function allocates a specified number of [b]contiguous bytes[/b] in memory.

[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" codeId="2121"]#include <stdlib.h>

int *ptr;
/* a block of 10 ints */
ptr = [b]malloc(10 * sizeof(*ptr))[/b];

if (ptr != NULL) {
  *(ptr + 2) = 50;  /* assign 50 to third int */
}[/code]
[b]malloc[/b] returns a pointer to the allocated memory. 
Notice that sizeof was applied to *ptr instead of int, making the code more robust should the *ptr declaration be changed to a different data type later.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

